    const size_t maxPoolSize = 100;
    LTEStatsPool statsPool(initialPoolSize, maxPoolSize);

    // Simulate network load processing
    for (size_t i = 0; i < 200; ++i) {
        processNetworkLoad(statsPool);
        std::this_thread::sleep_for(std::chrono::milliseconds(10));
    }

    return 0;
}
```

This code snippet provides a basic structure for a thread-safe LTE PHY statistics pool in C++. It includes a `LTEStatsPool` class that manages the allocation and reuse of `LTEPHY_Stats` structures using a mutex and condition variable to ensure thread safety. The `getStats` method waits until there are available stats, while `releaseStats` adds them back to the pool or deletes them if the pool is already at its maximum size.

The `processNetworkLoad` function demonstrates how to use the `LTEStatsPool` to process network loads by obtaining statistics from an external API, performing analysis, and releasing the statistics back into the pool. Error handling is included using a try-catch block to manage exceptions that may occur during API calls or processing.

This example serves as a foundation for further development and can be extended with additional features such as configurable resource limits, detailed logging, garbage collection strategies, and support for future network monitoring tools.']

